}
System.out.println(sum);', '["15", "25", "55", "30"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This sums the squares of 1 through 5: 1+4+9+16+25 = 55.', 'Control Structures', false, 'first', 2),
  ('cos-201-java-programming', 'COS 201: Computer Programming I (Java)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Agriculture', 'Computer Science', '200 Level', 'What is the output of the following code?
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) { if (i == 1) continue; if (i == 4) break; System.out.print(i + " ");
}', '["1 2 3", "2 3", "2 3 4", "1 2 3 4 5"]'::jsonb, 1, 'I=1: continue skips printing. i=2: prints "2 ". i=3: prints "3 ". i=4: break exits the loop entirely before printing. i=5 is never reached. Output: "2 3 ".', 'Control Structures', false, 'first', 2),
  ('cos-201-java-programming', 'COS 201: Computer Programming I (Java)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Agriculture', 'Computer Science', '200 Level', 'What is the value of `result` after this code runs?
int result = 1;
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) { result *= i;
}', '["15", "120", "20", "25"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This computes 5 factorial: result = 1*1*2*3*4*5 = 120.', 'Control Structures', false, 'first', 2),
  ('cos-201-java-programming', 'COS 201: Computer Programming I (Java)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Agriculture', 'Computer Science', '200 Level', 'Which keyword is used to declare an interface in Java?', '["Class", "Interface", "Abstract", "Implements"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Interface is the keyword used to declare an interface -- a contract of method signatures a class can agree to implement, e.g. `interface Drawable { void draw; }`.', 'Keywords', false, 'first', 2),
  ('cos-201-java-programming', 'COS 201: Computer Programming I (Java)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Agriculture', 'Computer Science', '200 Level', 'What is the fundamental difference between a class and an interface in Java, at a basic conceptual level?', '["A class can be instantiated directly with new and typically provides full implementations of its methods; an interface (traditionally) only declares method signatures that implementing classes must supply the actual code for", "There is no meaningful difference between them", "Interfaces can have instance variables that hold different values per object, exactly like classes", "A class can never have any methods"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A class is a full blueprint for objects, typically providing concrete method implementations, and can usually be instantiated directly. A traditional interface instead just declares a set of method signatures (a contract) that any implementing class must provide real code for.', 'Keywords', false, 'first', 2),
  ('cos-201-java-programming', 'COS 201: Computer Programming I (Java)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Agriculture', 'Computer Science', '200 Level', 'Which keyword marks a variable such that its value, once assigned, can never be changed again?', '["Static", "Final", "Private", "Void"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Final applied to a variable means it can only be assigned once; any later attempt to reassign it is a compile-time error.', 'Keywords', false, 'first', 2),
  ('cos-201-java-programming', 'COS 201: Computer Programming I (Java)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Agriculture', 'Computer Science', '200 Level', 'What is the range of values a single Java byte variable can hold?', '["0 to 255", "-128 to 127", "-32768 to 32767", "-2147483648 to 2147483647"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Java''s byte is an 8-bit signed type, giving it a range of -128 to 127 (not 0 to 255, which would be the range of an UNSIGNED 8-bit type -- Java''s byte is always signed).', 'Data Types', false, 'first', 2),
  ('cos-201-java-programming', 'COS 201: Computer Programming I (Java)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Agriculture', 'Computer Science', '200 Level', 'Which of these correctly declares a char variable holding the letter Z?', '["Char c = \"Z\";", "Char c = ''Z'';", "Char c = Z;", "Char c = (Z);"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Character literals use single quotes in Java: `char c = ''Z'';`. Double quotes would make "Z" a String literal instead, which cannot be assigned directly to a char variable.', 'Data Types', false, 'first', 2),
  ('cos-201-java-programming', 'COS 201: Computer Programming I (Java)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Agriculture', 'Computer Science', '200 Level', 'What is the output of `System.out.println(''A'' + 1);`?', '["''B''", "66", "A1", "A compile-time error"]'::jsonb, 1, 'When used in an arithmetic expression like this, the char ''A'' (Unicode/ASCII value 65) is automatically promoted to an int, so ''A'' + 1 evaluates to the int 66, which is what gets printed -- not the character ''B''.', 'Data Types', false, 'first', 2),
  ('cos-201-java-programming', 'COS 201: Computer Programming I (Java)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Agriculture', 'Computer Science', '200 Level', 'What is a Java array, in relation to the general concept of a ''data type''?', '["Arrays are unrelated to data types entirely", "An array is a reference type that holds a fixed-size collection of elements, all of the same declared type, such as int[] or String[]", "An array is a primitive type just like int", "Arrays can only ever hold boolean values"]'::jsonb, 1, 'An array is a reference (non-primitive) type in Java that groups together a fixed number of elements of one declared type -- e.g. int[] holds ints, String[] holds Strings -- distinct from Java''s eight primitive types themselves.', 'Data Types', false, 'first', 2),
  ('cos-201-java-programming', 'COS 201: Computer Programming I (Java)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Agriculture', 'Computer Science', '200 Level', 'What does the following pseudocode fragment compute, given N is a positive integer?
Set total = 0
Set i = 1
Repeat Set total = total + i Set i = i + 1
Until i > N
Display total', '["The sum of the integers from 1 to N", "The product of the integers from 1 to N", "The value of N itself, unchanged", "The count of even numbers up to N"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is a running-total accumulator: total starts at 0, and on each pass i (starting at 1) is added to total before i increments, repeating until i exceeds N -- so total ends up holding 1+2+..+N, the sum of integers from 1 to N.', 'Algorithm Development', false, 'first', 2),
  ('cos-201-java-programming', 'COS 201: Computer Programming I (Java)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Agriculture', 'Computer Science', '200 Level', 'What is the purpose of a ''trace table'' when developing or debugging an algorithm by hand?', '["It has no real purpose in program development", "It is a table recording the changing value of each relevant variable after every step of the algorithm, used to manually verify the algorithm''s logic produces correct results for given sample input", "It replaces the need to ever write actual code", "It only records the final output, never intermediate steps"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A trace table lists each variable as a column and each executed step as a row, recording how every variable''s value changes as the algorithm runs -- a systematic way to manually verify (or find bugs in) an algorithm''s logic before or without running real code.', 'Algorithm Development', false, 'first', 2),
  ('cos-201-java-programming', 'COS 201: Computer Programming I (Java)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Agriculture', 'Computer Science', '200 Level', 'In the Account class example, what would happen if `setName` were declared `private` instead of `public`?', '["Nothing would change; private and public behave identically for methods", "Code outside the Account class (such as the AccountTest class) would no longer be able to call setName directly on an Account object, causing a compile-time error at that call site", "The Account class itself could no longer use the setName method internally", "It would automatically make the name instance variable public instead"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Making setName private would restrict it to being callable only from within the Account class itself. Any attempt from AccountTest (a separate class) to call myAccount.setName(..) would then fail to compile, since private members are not accessible from outside their own class.', 'Classes, Objects and Methods', false, 'first', 2),
  ('cos-201-java-programming', 'COS 201: Computer Programming I (Java)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Agriculture', 'Computer Science', '200 Level', 'If class Account has instance variable `private String name;` with no explicit constructor, and class AccountTest does `Account a = new Account; a.name = "Ada";`, what happens?', '["This compiles and runs fine, directly setting the name", "This fails to compile, because name is private and cannot be accessed directly from outside the Account class -- AccountTest must instead call a public method like setName", "It automatically calls getName instead", "It silently does nothing, with no error at all"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Because name is declared private, it cannot be accessed directly (via dot notation) from any class other than Account itself -- attempting `a.name = "Ada";` from AccountTest is a compile-time error. The correct way to set it from outside is through a public method such as setName("Ada").', 'Classes, Objects and Methods', false, 'first', 2),
  ('cos-201-java-programming', 'COS 201: Computer Programming I (Java)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Agriculture', 'Computer Science', '200 Level', 'What is the output of the following code?
